          %h2#overview Overview
          %p
            Various phenomena in the context of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+geometry"} arithmetic geometry
            (and particularly in the context of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+groups"} algebraic groups
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+fields"} finite fields
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔽
                    %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}_q
            turn out to make perfect sense
            %em
              as expressions in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            when extrapolated to the case
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q=1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            with a single element (since in a field by definition the elements 1 and 0 are distinct).
          %p
            Motivated by such observations,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacques+Tits"} Jacques Tits
            envisioned in (
            %a{:href => "#Tits57"}> Tits 57
            ) a new kind of geometry adapted to the explanation of these identities.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christophe+Soul%C3%A9"} Christophe Soulé
            then expanded on Tits’ ideas by introducing the notion of
            %em field with one element
            and studying its fine arithmetic invariants. While there is no field with a single element in the standard sense of
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔽
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}_1
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔽
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}_1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi un
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_{un}
            (and is thus led to the inevitable pun).
          %p
            In the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+point+of+view"} relative point of view
            the
            = succeed "-schemes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed "-scheme" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spec%28Z%29"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Spec
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec(\mathbb{Z})
            %strong absolute algebraic geometry
            all “generalized schemes” should live over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec(F_1)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec(F_1)
            should live
            %strong below
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Spec
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec(\mathbb{Z})
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [*/G]
            live below the single point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
            (there is a direct image functor from sheaves on a point to sheaves over
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [*/G]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi un
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_{un}
            should lead to a natural proof of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+conjecture"} Riemann conjecture
            (see also MathOverflow
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/69389/riemann-hypothesis-via-absolute-geometry"} here
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Manin95"} Manin 95
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+hypothesis"} Riemann hypothesis
            might be solved by finding an
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔽
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}_1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analogy"} analogue
            of
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Weil"} André Weil
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+curves"} arithmetic curves
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+fields"} finite fields
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔽
                      %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}_q
          %p
            A first proposal for what a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variety"} variety
            “over
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔽
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}_1
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Soule"} Soulé 04
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#ConnesConsani08"} Connes-Consani 08
          %h2#BorgerAbsoluteGeometry Borger’s absolute geometry
          %p
            Maybe an emerging consensus is that the preferred approach is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borger%27s+absolute+geometry"} Borger's absolute geometry
            (
            %a{:href => "#Borger09"}> Borger 09
            ). Here the structure of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lambda-ring"} Lambda-ring
            on a ring
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Spec
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi Spec
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec(R) \to Spec(\mathbb{Z})
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+morphisms"} Frobenius morphisms
            and hence as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} descent
            data for descent to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔽
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec(\mathbb{F}_1)
            (which is defined thereby). This definition yields an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essential+geometric+morphism"} essential geometric morphism
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gros+topos"} gros
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/etale+toposes"} etale toposes
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Et
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mover
                      %munder
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mo ⟵
                        %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mrow
                  %mi Et
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔽
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Et(Spec(\mathbb{Z}))
                  \\stackrel{\overset{}{\longrightarrow}}{\stackrel{\overset{}{\longleftarrow}}{\underset{}{\longrightarrow}}}
                  Et(Spec(\mathbb{F}_1))
                  \\,,
          %p
            where on the right the notation is just suggestive, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            is a suitable one over
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lambda-rings"} Lambda-rings
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+image"} inverse image
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functor"} forgetful functor
            which forgets the Lambda structure, and its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+image"} direct image
            is given by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+jet+space"} arithmetic jet space
            construction (via the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+of+Witt+vectors"} ring of Witt vectors
            construction).
          %p
            This proposal seems to subsume many aspects of other existing proposals (see e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#LeBruyn13"} Le Bruyn 13
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+topos"} base topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Et
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔽
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Et(Spec(\mathbb{F}_1))
            which is rich and genuinely interesting in its own right.
